1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,265
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,265 --> 00:00:07,619
This is chapter two: the JVM runtime model.

3
00:00:08,069 --> 00:00:12,840
You can write Java for years without a model of the runtime underneath it.

4
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:19,482
Then a container gets killed with a healthy-looking heap, or a benchmark lies to you, and you need one.

5
00:00:20,382 --> 00:00:23,050
Three failures with the same root cause.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:26,918
First, a container killed with exit code one thirty-seven.

7
00:00:26,918 --> 00:00:33,988
The heap was capped at two gigabytes in a two gigabyte container, and it never went above one point eight.

8
00:00:33,988 --> 00:00:38,323
The kernel killed it anyway, because the heap is not the process.

9
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:45,856
Second, two errors that look the same and aren't: ClassNotFoundException, and NoClassDefFoundError.

10
00:00:46,306 --> 00:00:53,394
And third, a benchmark where the same code on the same data gets a hundred and fifty times faster.

11
00:00:53,394 --> 00:00:58,167
Nothing changed, except how many times the JVM had watched it run.

12
00:00:59,067 --> 00:01:01,686
Here are the JVM's memory regions.

13
00:01:01,686 --> 00:01:08,155
The heap holds every object and array, and it's the only thing minus X M X controls.

14
00:01:08,605 --> 00:01:14,429
Metaspace holds class metadata, in native memory, and it's unbounded by default.

15
00:01:14,879 --> 00:01:18,527
Every thread has its own stack, about a megabyte each.

16
00:01:18,977 --> 00:01:22,532
The code cache holds JIT-compiled machine code.

17
00:01:22,532 --> 00:01:24,877
When it fills, nothing crashes.

18
00:01:24,877 --> 00:01:28,129
The application just gets slower, silently.

19
00:01:28,579 --> 00:01:33,273
And direct memory holds direct byte buffers and foreign memory segments.

20
00:01:33,723 --> 00:01:40,160
So the single most useful fact in this chapter: the heap flag bounds the heap, not the process.

21
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:45,648
The real footprint is all of these added together, plus the JVM's own structures.

22
00:01:46,548 --> 00:01:49,536
Add it up for that two gigabyte container.

23
00:01:49,536 --> 00:01:57,149
Two gigabytes of heap, plus metaspace, code cache, two hundred thread stacks, and the JVM's own structures.

24
00:01:57,149 --> 00:02:01,417
Around two point six gigabytes, in a two gigabyte container.

25
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:04,462
Instead, let the JVM size itself.

26
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:11,538
Container support has been on by default since Java 10, so the JVM reads the cgroup limit.

27
00:02:11,538 --> 00:02:17,671
MaxRAMPercentage of about seventy leaves room for everything outside the heap.

28
00:02:17,671 --> 00:02:19,873
Then tune with measurements.

29
00:02:20,323 --> 00:02:26,708
To see where native memory goes, turn on native memory tracking and ask jcmd for a summary.

30
00:02:26,708 --> 00:02:32,461
The gap between the heap and the total is what your container sizing has to cover.

31
00:02:33,361 --> 00:02:36,541
Every class goes through the same pipeline.

32
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:44,010
It's loaded, verified, prepared, resolved, and finally initialised, when its static initialisers run.

33
00:02:44,010 --> 00:02:48,743
Initialisation is lazy: it happens on first use, not at startup.

34
00:02:49,193 --> 00:02:51,351
Which explains the two errors.

35
00:02:51,351 --> 00:02:58,043
ClassNotFoundException is a checked exception from an explicit lookup like Class dot forName.

36
00:02:58,043 --> 00:03:05,525
NoClassDefFoundError comes from the JVM itself: the class existed at compile time but can't be used now.

37
00:03:05,525 --> 00:03:08,116
Usually that's a missing dependency.

38
00:03:08,116 --> 00:03:12,361
But it can also mean the class's static initialiser failed.

39
00:03:13,261 --> 00:03:15,572
Here's the trap that wastes hours.

40
00:03:15,572 --> 00:03:22,370
A class loads its config in a static field initialiser, and the file is missing in this environment.

41
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:27,988
The first use throws ExceptionInInitializerError, with the real cause.

42
00:03:27,988 --> 00:03:31,753
But the class is now marked erroneous, permanently.

43
00:03:31,753 --> 00:03:37,881
Every use after that reports only NoClassDefFoundError: could not initialise class.

44
00:03:38,331 --> 00:03:44,855
So when you see that message, search backwards in the log for the first ExceptionInInitializerError.

45
00:03:44,855 --> 00:03:46,551
That's where the cause is.

46
00:03:46,551 --> 00:03:49,617
And don't do heavy work in static initialisers.

47
00:03:50,517 --> 00:03:52,685
Class loaders form a hierarchy.

48
00:03:52,685 --> 00:03:59,399
Your application loader, the platform loader, and the bootstrap loader that holds java dot base.

49
00:03:59,399 --> 00:04:01,567
Each one asks its parent first.

50
00:04:02,017 --> 00:04:08,128
Parent-first delegation is why you can't shadow java dot lang dot String with your own.

51
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:17,399
And it's why two copies of a library on the classpath resolve to whichever the application loader finds first: the classic JAR hell.

52
00:04:17,849 --> 00:04:21,124
A class's identity is its name plus its loader.

53
00:04:21,124 --> 00:04:25,514
The same bytes loaded by two loaders are two different classes.

54
00:04:25,514 --> 00:04:27,744
So Order can't be cast to Order.

55
00:04:27,744 --> 00:04:31,438
Baffling until you know the rule, obvious afterwards.

56
00:04:32,338 --> 00:04:34,144
Now the lying benchmark.

57
00:04:34,144 --> 00:04:36,477
Code starts in the interpreter.

58
00:04:36,477 --> 00:04:44,455
After a few hundred calls, the C1 compiler produces quick, lightly optimised code with profiling counters.

59
00:04:44,455 --> 00:04:48,594
After about ten thousand, C2 produces the fast version.

60
00:04:49,044 --> 00:04:52,641
C2 optimises using assumptions from that profile.

61
00:04:52,641 --> 00:04:58,440
That a call site only ever sees one implementation, or a branch is never taken.

62
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:05,413
If an assumption is later violated, the compiled code is thrown away, and execution falls back.

63
00:05:05,413 --> 00:05:07,028
That's deoptimisation.

64
00:05:07,478 --> 00:05:10,715
You can watch it happen with PrintCompilation.

65
00:05:10,715 --> 00:05:14,655
Tier four is C2, and made not entrant means deoptimised.

66
00:05:14,655 --> 00:05:22,113
If one method keeps cycling through that, something on its call path keeps breaking the JIT's assumptions.

67
00:05:22,563 --> 00:05:27,233
So any benchmark without a warmup phase measures the interpreter.

68
00:05:27,233 --> 00:05:28,813
That's why JMH exists.

69
00:05:29,713 --> 00:05:31,043
A few quiet costs.

70
00:05:31,043 --> 00:05:36,509
Metaspace is reclaimed only when a whole class loader becomes unreachable.

71
00:05:36,509 --> 00:05:44,191
One lingering reference keeps every class that loader defined alive, which is the classic redeploy leak.

72
00:05:44,191 --> 00:05:46,481
So cap metaspace and fail fast.

73
00:05:46,931 --> 00:05:49,425
Escape analysis cuts the other way.

74
00:05:49,425 --> 00:05:54,128
If an object never leaves a method, the JIT may never allocate it.

75
00:05:54,128 --> 00:06:01,609
A short-lived Optional often costs nothing, so avoid-allocation advice needs measurement, not assumption.

76
00:06:02,059 --> 00:06:03,370
And object layout.

77
00:06:03,370 --> 00:06:09,779
Every object has a header: twelve bytes, or eight with Java 25's compact object headers.

78
00:06:09,779 --> 00:06:17,717
References are four bytes thanks to compressed oops, which only works up to about a thirty-two gigabyte heap.

79
00:06:18,617 --> 00:06:19,885
Production rules.

80
00:06:19,885 --> 00:06:26,371
Size containers for the whole process, using MaxRAMPercentage rather than a fixed heap.

81
00:06:26,371 --> 00:06:30,099
Cap metaspace, and dump the heap on out-of-memory.

82
00:06:30,099 --> 00:06:34,199
Turn on native memory tracking where footprint matters.

83
00:06:34,199 --> 00:06:37,703
Use jcmd as your single diagnostic entry point.

84
00:06:37,703 --> 00:06:40,015
And warm up before you measure.

85
00:06:40,465 --> 00:06:43,931
Never set the heap equal to the container limit.

86
00:06:43,931 --> 00:06:48,986
Never raise the stack size before confirming the recursion is bounded.

87
00:06:48,986 --> 00:06:52,885
Never assume NoClassDefFoundError means a missing JAR.

88
00:06:52,885 --> 00:06:55,196
Never ignore code cache is full.

89
00:06:55,196 --> 00:06:58,662
And never do heavy work in a static initialiser.

90
00:06:59,562 --> 00:07:00,208
To recap.

91
00:07:00,208 --> 00:07:06,091
The heap flag bounds the heap, not the process, so size containers for everything.

92
00:07:06,091 --> 00:07:11,832
A failed static initialiser poisons its class for good, so read the first error.

93
00:07:11,832 --> 00:07:15,204
A class's identity is its name plus its loader.

94
00:07:15,204 --> 00:07:23,455
And code moves from interpreter to C1 to C2, and back again on deoptimisation, which is why benchmarks need warmup.

95
00:07:23,905 --> 00:07:26,246
Next is chapter three: the toolchain.

96
00:07:26,246 --> 00:07:27,511
Thanks for watching.
